          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a dry cough for about ten days after a cold, no fever. Should I be doing anything?",
          "answer": "**If you have chest pain, or trouble breathing right now, call emergency services immediately. Do not drive yourself.** The urgency tool flagged this as an emergency, but that came from the words \"chest pain\" in my search, which were a checklist of warning signs, not something you reported. Please check with yourself: do you have chest pain or breathlessness now?\n\n**About the cough:** The common cold guidance says cough often lasts two to three weeks after a cold. It says this is normal and does not by itself mean a bacterial infection. Ten days without fever fits that pattern (Upper respiratory infection, common cold). Flu guidance says cough can last two weeks or more, so a clinician may still want to see you if it doesn't settle.\n\n**Get medical review promptly (same day or through your GP) if:**\n- Your cough is getting worse, or you improved and then got worse again. The flu guidance describes this pattern as one worth recognizing, because it can mean a secondary chest infection.\n- The cough is not improving by about three weeks.\n- You cough up blood. My retrieved sources don't cover this, so I can't tell you how urgent it is. Please contact a clinician promptly.\n\n**Seek emergency care if you develop:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or sudden breathlessness.\n- Being unable to speak in full sentences.\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face or fingertips.\n- Chest pain with breathlessness.\n\nLack of fever is reassuring, but the sepsis guidance says not to rely on a normal temperature if you feel seriously unwell.\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis. A clinician who examines you can tell you what is going on.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
